<h1>Video 093 — Daemon Threads</h1>
<h2>Video info</h2>
<p>Title: Core Java with OCJP/SCJP: Multi Threading Part-13 || Daemon Threads</p>
<p>Source: real YouTube auto-captions (.transcripts/093-u5OunWJhKoE.txt). ASR decoded in place: demand / demon / deand / remon = daemon; threats = threads; garb / carb / turb collector = garbage collector; attach legioner / attachation / attach legar = Attach Listener; signal dispatcher = Signal Dispatcher; is a demand / is remon = isDaemon(); set a demand / set demand = setDaemon(); illegal third state / illegal Legal thread state = IllegalThreadStateException; Nan = non-daemon; JM / JDM = JVM; keep writing board headings from spoken “keeping demand threads.”</p>
<p>This lecture defines daemon threads, movie-shooting analogy for their purpose, GC priority raise/lower story, isDaemon/setDaemon API and the “only before start” rule, default nature / inheritance, impossibility of changing main’s daemon nature, then the critical rule that when the last non-daemon dies all daemons are killed — proved with DaemonThreadDemo.</p>
<h3>00:09 — Topic: Daemon threads</h3>
<p>Board: Daemon threads. Important for interview entertainment / interview room.</p>
<h3>00:30 — Definition + examples</h3>
<p>Definition: The thread which is executing in the background is called a daemon thread.</p>
<p>Best examples (system-level):</p>
<ol><li>Garbage collector</li></ol>
<ol><li>Attach Listener</li></ol>
<ol><li>Signal Dispatcher</li></ol>
<pre class='code'>// Board:
 // The threads which are executing in the background
 // are called daemon threads
 // Examples: Garbage collector, Signal dispatcher, Attach listener etc.</pre>
<h3>02:46 — Advantage / objective / purpose (movie analogy begins)</h3>
<p>Purpose of daemon threads — long cinema / shooting-spot analogy:</p>
<ul><li>On a 70 mm screen you see hero, heroine, character artists.</li></ul>
<ul><li>To put those people on screen, huge background staff is required (producer, director, makeup, music director, choreographer, food, crowd control, buses, balloon sellers, ice-cream vendors walking in the park scene, etc.).</li></ul>
<ul><li>On screen maybe 2 people talking in a park; surrounding support may be 100+ members / many buses.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Producer / director / makeup rarely appear on screen, but without them the shoot cannot run.</li></ul>
<p>Mapping:</p>
<p>Main purpose of daemon threads: provide support for non-daemon threads.</p>
<h3>08:34 — Concrete JVM example with garbage collector</h3>
<p>Main thread is working. Suddenly main faces a memory problem. JVM runs the garbage collector (daemon) — may raise its priority, destroy useless objects, free memory — so main can continue.</p>
<p>Who provided required memory for main? Indirectly the GC. Purpose of GC: support the main / non-daemon thread.</p>
<pre class='code'> // Board (dictated):
 // The main objective of daemon threads is
 // to provide support for non-daemon threads (main thread)</pre>
<p>Example flow dictated:</p>
<pre class='code'> // If main thread runs with low memory
 // then JVM runs garbage collector
 // to destroy useless objects
 // so that number of bytes of free memory will be improved
 // With this free memory main thread can continue its execution</pre>
<h3>12:08 — Priority of daemon threads</h3>
<p>Usually daemon threads run with low priority.</p>
<p>Story:</p>
<ul><li>Assume main priority 5, GC priority 1 → main keeps executing.</li></ul>
<ul><li>If memory problem: JVM may raise GC priority from 1 to 10 so GC can reclaim memory.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Once enough free memory: JVM reduces GC priority back to 1 → main continues.</li></ul>
<p>Important twist: Usually daemons have low priority, but based on requirement a daemon can run with high priority also. Do not say “daemon always low priority.”</p>
<pre class='code'> // Board:
 // Usually daemon threads having low priority
 // But based on our requirement
 // daemon threads can run with high priority also</pre>
<h3>14:26 — How to check / change daemon nature (API)</h3>
<p>Two methods on Thread:</p>
<ol><li>public boolean isDaemon() — check whether thread is daemon.</li></ol>
<ol><li>public void setDaemon(boolean b) — true → become daemon; false → become non-daemon.</li></ol>
<h3>15:41 — Twist: change only BEFORE start</h3>
<p>Changing daemon nature is possible only before starting the thread.</p>
<p>If thread already start()ed and you call setDaemon(...) → runtime exception:</p>
<pre class='code'> // IllegalThreadStateException
public boolean isDaemon();
public void setDaemon(boolean b);

 // Changing daemon nature is possible before starting of a thread only
 // After starting a thread, if we try to change daemon nature
 // → RuntimeException: IllegalThreadStateException</pre>
<h3>19:55 — Default nature</h3>
<ul><li>By default main thread is always non-daemon.</li></ul>
<ul><li>For all remaining threads, daemon nature is inherited from parent to child:</li></ul>
<ul><li>If parent is daemon → child is daemon.</li></ul>
<ul><li>If parent is non-daemon → child is non-daemon.</li></ul>
<pre class='code'> // Board:
 // By default main thread is always non-daemon
 // And for all remaining threads
 // daemon nature will be inherited from parent to child
 // If parent is daemon → child is also daemon
 // If parent is non-daemon → child is also non-daemon</pre>
<h3>23:02 — Can we change daemon nature of main?</h3>
<p>Impossible.</p>
<p>Reason: main is already started by the JVM at the very beginning. Programmer never starts main. When control reaches programmer code, main has already started → setDaemon on main → IllegalThreadStateException.</p>
<p>For threads you create: change daemon nature before start() — no problem.</p>
<pre class='code'> // Board:
 // It is impossible to change daemon nature of main thread
 // because it is already started by JVM at the beginning</pre>
<h3>25:07 — Live demo sketch: check main, fail to set main, succeed on child</h3>
<pre class='code'>class MyThread extends Thread {
}

class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // At this point: one thread = main
        System.out.println(Thread.currentThread().isDaemon());
        // prints false

        Thread.currentThread().setDaemon(true);
        // CE? No — compiles, but at runtime:
        // Exception in thread &quot;main&quot; java.lang.IllegalThreadStateException

        MyThread t = new MyThread(); // parent main = non-daemon → child non-daemon
        System.out.println(t.isDaemon());
        // prints false

        t.setDaemon(true); // OK — not started yet
        System.out.println(t.isDaemon());
        // prints true
    }
}</pre>
<p>He compiles/runs stepwise:</p>
<ol><li>Only isDaemon on current thread → false.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Add setDaemon(true) on current thread → IllegalThreadStateException.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Create child, print isDaemon → false; setDaemon(true) then print → true.</li></ol>
<h3>32:02 — Board form of the small Test example (outputs beside SOPs)</h3>
<pre class='code'>class MyThread extends Thread {
}

class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(Thread.currentThread().isDaemon());
        // prints false

        Thread.currentThread().setDaemon(true);
        // IllegalThreadStateException

        MyThread t = new MyThread();
        System.out.println(t.isDaemon());
        // prints false

        t.setDaemon(true);
        System.out.println(t.isDaemon());
        // prints true
    }
}</pre>
<h3>33:32 — Last major point: when last non-daemon ends, daemons die</h3>
<p>Return to purpose: daemons support non-daemons.</p>
<p>Shooting completed / actors left → is makeup person still required? No. Cameraman? No.</p>
<p>So: once the last non-daemon thread completes, daemon threads are no longer required.</p>
<p>Rule: Whenever the last non-daemon thread terminates, all daemon threads will be terminated automatically, irrespective of their position (middle of work / beginning / end — they are killed).</p>
<pre class='code'> // Board (dictated):
 // Whenever last non-daemon thread terminates
 // automatically all daemon threads will be terminated
 // irrespective of their position</pre>
<h3>36:42 — Program: `DaemonThreadDemo` (child loop + optional setDaemon)</h3>
<pre class='code'>class MyThread extends Thread {
    public void run() {
        for (int i = 0; i &lt; 10; i++) {
            System.out.println(&quot;Child Thread&quot;);
            try {
                Thread.sleep(2000);
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            }
        }
    }
}

class DaemonThreadDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        MyThread t = new MyThread();
        // Line 1:
        t.setDaemon(true);
        t.start();
        System.out.println(&quot;End of main thread&quot;);
    }
}</pre>
<h3>39:33 — Case A: comment Line 1 (`setDaemon`)</h3>
<ul><li>Main = non-daemon, child = non-daemon (inherited).</li></ul>
<ul><li>Both execute until their own completion.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Child prints “Child Thread” 10 times even after “End of main thread”.</li></ul>
<pre class='code'>// Typical when setDaemon commented:
End of main thread
Child Thread
Child Thread
... (10 times total)</pre>
<h3>40:10 — Case B: Line 1 active (`t.setDaemon(true)`)</h3>
<ul><li>Main = non-daemon, child = daemon.</li></ul>
<ul><li>When main terminates, child is terminated automatically — child will not reliably finish 10 iterations.</li></ul>
<p>He runs and shows: often only End of main thread, maybe zero or one “Child Thread” — no guarantee even for one print. Child does not execute 10 times.</p>
<pre class='code'>// Possible outputs when daemon:
End of main thread
// or
End of main thread
Child Thread
// Child does NOT run 10 times</pre>
<h3>46:14 — Board theory beside the program</h3>
<p>If commenting line 1 (`setDaemon`):</p>
<ul><li>Both main and child are non-daemon.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Hence both threads execute until their completion.</li></ul>
<p>If not commenting line 1:</p>
<ul><li>Main is non-daemon and child is daemon.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Hence whenever main terminates, automatically child will be terminated.</li></ul>
<p>In daemon case, possible outputs:</p>
<ul><li>End of main thread followed by at most ~one Child Thread, or only End of main thread.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Child executed 10 times? No — zero times or max one time typically.</li></ul>
<h3>49:52 — Final conclusion</h3>
<p>Whenever the last non-daemon thread terminates, automatically every daemon thread will be terminated by default. End of Part-13.</p>
